course: adult-life
lesson: 11
title: When things break: boilers, plumbers and paint
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A home is like a big machine with lots of parts: pipes, wires, a boiler, walls, a roof and bins that need emptying, and every part breaks or wears out sooner or later.
explain: Some jobs grown-ups do themselves, which is called DIY, and some need a trained person like a plumber or an electrician, who is busy and costs a lot.
explain: In a rented home the landlord owns the house and must fix the big things, but tenants often have to ask again and again before anything happens.
explain: Picture a cold January evening in Manchester: Mum bangs the boiler and says "It's packed in again", Dad sighs "the plumber can't come till Thursday, and it'll cost an arm and a leg", and everyone boils the kettle for a wash.
explain: That is why grown-ups sound cross about houses: repairs cost money, take time and turn the home upside down, but the trouble is almost always fixed in the end.
fact: The boiler heats the water and the radiators, and "packed in" means it has stopped working.
fact: A plumber fixes pipes, taps and toilets; "it'll cost an arm and a leg" only means it will be very expensive.
fact: DIY means "do it yourself": fixing or building things at home instead of paying someone, which saves money but sometimes goes wrong and costs more.
fact: Wiring must be done by a trained electrician, because mistakes with electricity can cause fires and shocks, and there are laws about it.
fact: Having the kitchen redone means builders take the old one out and put a new one in, so for a while there is nowhere to cook and families eat takeaway.
fact: Big building jobs often take longer and cost more than planned.
fact: Damp is when walls get wet inside and grow black mould; in a rented home the landlord is supposed to fix it, and some are slow to do so.
fact: Tenants who cannot get a repair done may complain more than once or ask the council for help.
fact: One coat of paint often looks patchy, so a second coat is painted on once the first is dry.
fact: Arguing over paint colours like "Magnolia" or "Duck Egg Blue" is a small disagreement about taste, not a real fight, and it ends with a choice or a compromise.
fact: In houses joined together, sound travels through the walls, so grown-ups first knock and ask politely and only later complain to the landlord or the council.
fact: The council collects rubbish on set days, with separate bins for general rubbish and recycling, and a missed bin day means rubbish piles up for a week or two.
exercise: Mum says "The boiler's packed in again". What does she mean? | The boiler has stopped working | The boiler has been packed into a box | The boiler is being moved to a new house | The boiler has been switched to summer mode
exercise: Dad says the plumber will "cost an arm and a leg". What does it mean? | It will be very expensive | The plumber is going to hurt someone | The plumber only charges for body parts | It will be cheap but slow
exercise: What does a plumber fix? | Pipes, taps and toilets | Cars and lorries | Roofs and chimneys only | Phones and laptops
exercise: What does DIY stand for? | Do it yourself | Don't involve yourself | Done in a year | Doors, ironing and yards
exercise: Why can DIY sometimes end up costing more? | If it goes wrong, someone has to be paid to fix it anyway | Because tools always cost far more than paying a plumber to come round | Because the council charges people for doing DIY | Because DIY always takes ten times longer than builders
exercise: Why should only an electrician do the wiring? | Mistakes with electricity can cause fires and shocks | Electricians are the only people allowed to own screwdrivers | Wires are too heavy for other people to lift | Electricity only works for people who trained
exercise: "We're having the kitchen redone." Why does the family eat takeaway? | The old kitchen is out, so there is nowhere to cook | Takeaway is a rule while builders are in the house | The builders eat all the food in the fridge | The cooker gets a holiday while the walls are painted
exercise: Why do grown-ups get fed up during big building jobs? | They often take longer and cost more than planned | Builders always break the law on purpose | The family has to pay the builders' rent | Building jobs are always finished in a single day
exercise: What is damp in a house? | Walls getting wet inside and growing black mould | Washing left out in the rain | A cold room with the window open | A floor that has just been mopped
exercise: Who should fix the damp in a rented home? | The landlord | The children who live there | The next-door neighbours | The postman
exercise: Why does Mum say a wall "needs a second coat"? | One layer of paint looks patchy, so another goes on when it is dry | The wall is cold and needs a jacket | The first paint was the wrong colour on purpose, to test it out on the wall | Paint always has to be done twice by law
exercise: Mum and Dad argue for ages over "Magnolia" or "Duck Egg Blue". What is going on? | They disagree about a paint colour for the hall | They are arguing about which flowers to plant | They are choosing names for a new pet | They are fighting about where to go on holiday
exercise: "The neighbours' music is going through the wall." What do grown-ups usually do first? | Knock and ask politely | Call the police straight away | Play their own music louder | Move to a new house
exercise: Mum says "The bins go out on Tuesday night, and it's recycling week." Why does it matter? | If the bins miss the collection, rubbish piles up for a week or two | The council fines every family that puts its bins out on the wrong night of the week | Recycling bins are only for Tuesdays forever | Bins left in overnight explode
exercise: Who collects the rubbish from the bins? | The council, on set days | The landlord, whenever asked | The neighbours, in turns | The supermarket, with the shopping
exercise: Dad on a ladder says "how hard can it be?" What is he doing? | Trying a repair himself instead of paying someone | Asking a real question to the plumber | Telling the children to fix it | Giving up and calling an electrician
test: You overhear Dad on the phone: "Thursday? Seriously? Fine, put me down... and how much? Right." Then he hangs up and groans. What has most likely happened? | He has booked a plumber who can't come soon and will be expensive | He has won a prize that arrives on Thursday | He has been told off by his boss for being late to work three times this week | He has ordered a takeaway for Thursday
test: my sister says "arm and a leg" means the plumber cuts people up lol. right? | No, it just means something costs a lot of money | Yes, plumbers sometimes do that if you don't pay | Yes, but only in old stories | No, it means the plumber is very tall
test: dad's up the ladder with a drill going "how hard can it be" and mum's like "oh god here we go". why's mum worried | DIY can go wrong and end up costing more | Mum wants to do the DIY herself first | Ladders are against the law in houses | Drills only work for proper electricians
test: There's a burnt smell from a plug and a crackle. Your big brother says "I'll sort it, pass me a knife." What is right? | Stop him and tell a grown-up, this is a job for an electrician | Pass him the knife, it is quick and the plug is only a little bit hot | Pour water on the plug to cool it | Unscrew it yourself while he holds the torch
test: bloody builders said 2 weeks, it's been 5 and we're STILL eating chips out of paper. what's going on | The kitchen job is taking longer than planned, which is common | The family has decided to eat chips forever | The builders are on a five-week holiday | Takeaway is cheaper than cooking, so the family chose it and stopped the job
test: On the news: "Thousands of tenants say their landlords ignore mould." What does the news story mean? | People renting homes say their landlords are not fixing damp | Landlords are complaining about their tenants' cooking making the walls wet | Mould has been found in supermarket food | Tenants are growing mould on purpose
test: Gran: "That landlord's useless, three letters and still no one's come about the damp." What might the family do next? | Ask the council for help | Paint over the mould and forget it | Stop paying for the water | Fix the walls with sticky tape
test: Mum painted the bedroom yesterday and it looks stripy. Your brother says "she did it wrong, ha". Put him right. | One coat often looks patchy, it needs a second coat when dry | She used the wrong kind of brush on purpose to make a pattern of stripes | Stripy paint means the wall is damp | It will stay stripy forever now
test: Mum and Dad have been arguing about "Duck Egg" for an hour. Your little sister cries "are you getting divorced?" What is true? | No, it is just two people with different tastes about a colour | Yes, arguing about paint is a sign of a big split | Yes, Duck Egg is a code word for a serious fight | No, they are arguing about what to cook for tea
test: It's 1am, thump thump through the wall, Dad goes "right, that's it". What is he most likely about to do? | Go and knock next door and ask them to turn it down | Phone the plumber to fix the wall | Turn on his own music to drown it out forever | Pack the family's bags and move house
test: "crap, it's Wednesday morning and the bins are still in the drive". what happens now | The rubbish stays until the next collection, maybe one or two weeks | The council will come back this afternoon specially for your bins | The bins empty themselves at the end of the day | The neighbours have to take your rubbish
test: Which of these is a job the landlord, not the tenant, is supposed to sort out? | Fixing the damp and the broken heating | Taking the bins out on bin night | Choosing the paint colour in the kids' room | Washing up after tea
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a householder is advised against undertaking electrical wiring personally. | Errors in such work may occasion fire or electric shock | Electrical components are prohibited from sale to householders | Such work invariably requires a period of several months | Wiring is the sole responsibility of the refuse collection service
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning dampness in a rented dwelling. | The remedy thereof is ordinarily the obligation of the landlord | The remedy thereof is ordinarily the obligation of the tenant's children | Dampness is of no consequence to the health of occupants | Dampness is remedied solely by the application of paint
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the expression "it will cost an arm and a leg"? | The expenditure incurred will be very considerable | The tradesman shall require payment in bodily injury | The work shall be performed without charge | The expenditure incurred will be negligible
exam: It is given that the refuse of a household is not presented for collection upon the designated evening. Ascertain the most probable consequence. | The refuse shall remain until the subsequent scheduled collection | The council shall return on the following morning without fail to collect it | The household shall be required to vacate the dwelling | The refuse shall be removed by the landlord forthwith
